<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ténès</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ténès is a port town on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a>, in Chlef Province west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algiers">Algiers</a>. Founded by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoenicians">Phoenicians</a> as Cartennae, it became a Roman colony, and in the 9th and 10th centuries it was held by princes of the Sulaymanid branch of the Idrisid family. The town was later a minor port under Zayyanid, Ottoman and French rule.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tenes.jpg" alt="Ténès" width="320" height="220" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: تنس في صورة · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Skyview-tenes.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Ténès is a town on the coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a>, around 200 kilometres west of the capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algiers">Algiers</a>. Its name comes from the Berber TNS, meaning camping. In antiquity the local tribes were known, according to Ptolemy, as the Bakoyta. Its harbour served for centuries as an outlet for the fruit and grain of the fertile land around it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Ténès was founded as a Phoenician port in or before the 8th century BCE. Like other Phoenician harbours it fell under the hegemony of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> around the 6th century BCE and of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punic-wars">Punic Wars</a>. Its Punic name became Cartenna or Cartennae, a plural that suggests a separate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> settlement nearby. It rose to colony status under the empire. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vandals">Vandals</a> sacked it during their conquest of Roman North Africa. It was then reconquered by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> and taken by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad Caliphate</a>, after which it disappeared except for a mostly ruined fortification.</p><p>Medieval Ténès was founded by Spaniards in the 9th century, and al-Bakri dates it to 875 or 876 (262 AH). They set up their base 3.2 kilometres from the former settlement and invited settlers from Elvira and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murcia">Murcia</a>, but many left because of outbreaks of fever. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berbers</a> from Suk Ibrahim on the Chelif replaced them, and the city prospered despite its unhealthy climate. A local dynasty claiming descent from Ali ruled for a time. After the 10th century it passed in turn under Fatimid, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanhaja">Sanhaja</a>, Maghrawa, Almoravid, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohad</a> and Ziyanid control. In the later 15th century it prospered amid the exodus of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moors">Moors</a> after the War of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oruc-reis">Oruç Reis</a> sacked Ténès in 1517, and his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hayreddin-barbarossa">Hayreddin Barbarossa</a> conquered it for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> a few years later. It received a garrison, but its economic life gradually collapsed, and its European grain trade was gone by the 18th century. Following the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-invasion-of-algiers">French invasion of Algiers</a> in 1830 the town came under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir-abdelkader">Emir Abdelkader</a>, who tried to revive its port, and the locals surrendered to the French without a fight in 1843.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Modern Ténès was established by the French in 1847 on the ruins of the Phoenician and Roman colony. French writers had at first assumed that ancient Cartennae lay at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mostaganem">Mostaganem</a>, until engravings found by archaeologists settled the identification. The harbour, about 1.5 kilometres away, first served as a port for goods from the Chelif and declined after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algiers">Algiers</a>–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oran">Oran</a> railway was built. The town has Phoenician and Roman tombs, prehistoric caves at Sidi Merouane, the Great Mosque of Sidi Ahmed Boumaza and a lighthouse.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ténès” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The emirate was divided among family members, who ruled towns such as Tlemcen, Arshgul and <b>Ténès</b>, relying on local Zenata Berber tribes.</blockquote><a href="/state/sulaymanid-emirate">Sulaymanid emirate</a></li></ul></section>
